{
  "proposal_nominal": { "wife": 770000, "husband": 770000 },
  "proposal_after_tax": {
    "wife": 613008,
    "husband": 662000,
    "assumptions": "Wife = house equity 700,000 minus 6% selling costs (66,000) minus tax on hypothetical sale today: gain 1,100,000 - 66,000 - 300,000 basis = 734,000, less 250,000 single §121 exclusion (26 U.S.C. §121(b)(1), §1041 carryover basis) = 484,000 taxed at 15% LTCG + 3.8% NIIT (engagement assumption; the gain itself pushes MAGI over the 200,000 single NIIT threshold) = 90,992 -> 543,008; plus cash 50,000 + Volvo 20,000 = 613,008. Husband = 401(k) 400,000 pre-tax at 24% marginal (stated assumption; at 12% his side is 710,000/gap 96,992; at 32% his side is 630,000/gap 16,992) = 304,000; brokerage 300,000 minus 15% on embedded gain 80,000 (12,000; +3,040 if NIIT applies) = 288,000; cash 50,000 + Tacoma 20,000 = 662,000. Nominal 'equal halves' leave wife 613,008/662,000 = 92.6% of husband, a gap of 48,992. Excludes the undisclosed bonus (below), which is itself marital property; client confirms bonuses recur (prior year ~40,000, her report)."
  },
  "undisclosed_income": {
    "found": true,
    "amount": 45000,
    "source": "Granite Peak HR letter 2026-03-02 documents a 2026 target bonus of $45,000 (50% paid Sept 2026, 50% Feb 2027; at 112% of plan the expected payout is $50,400), contradicting the sworn affidavit of 2026-08-30 ('none expected for 2026'); the 2026-09-15 pay stub shows YTD gross $146,212 vs $119,000 salary prorated to that date, a ~$27,212 excess roughly matching the September half. Client confirms Husband 'always' gets a bonus; prior year ~$40,000 (client-reported, not yet documented - request W-2/bonus records for 2024-2026 in discovery). Columbia treats expected or recurring bonuses evidenced by employer documents as income (library/columbia_child_support_guidelines.md); non-disclosure in the sworn affidavit exposes Husband to reopening, sanctions, and an unequal division in Wife's favor (library/columbia_equitable_distribution.md, Col. R. Fam. P. 16.2(e)). Open: whether the $100,000 schedule cash already includes the Sept payment; the Feb 2027 half ($22,500-$25,200) is not on the schedule at all."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "name": "A2 (primary - fits client's 3-6 year goal, NO refinance required): deferred joint sale; Husband stays on title/mortgage; each spouse later claims his/her own $250,000 §121 exclusion",
      "wife_after_tax": 680327,
      "husband_after_tax": 680327,
      "instruments": ["decree term: fixed sale date/milestone, Wife occupies, 50/50 net proceeds, credit to whoever pays principal (~24,221 paydown over 36 months of 2,648 P&I)", "§121(d)(3)(A) deemed-use keeps Husband's $250k exclusion alive while Wife occupies under the decree (library/irc_1041_121_71.md): gain 734,000 sheltered to 234,000 (117,000 each) -> her tax 17,550 (MAGI 72,000+117,000=189,000 < 200,000 NIIT threshold; maintenance non-includible post-TCJA, child support never taxable), his 21,996; total 39,546 vs 43,992 for an immediate joint-return sale; net 594,454, each 297,227 (valued at today's price)", "after-tax estate = 594,454 + 304,000 (401k) + 288,000 (brokerage) + 100,000 (cash) + 40,000 (vehicles) + 34,200 (bonus at 0.76) = 1,360,654; half = 680,327", "QDRO ~134,211 nominal to Wife (680,327 - her base 578,327 = 102,000 after-tax / 0.76)", "consider restructuring maintenance as Husband paying the housing cost directly (2,648 P&I > 2,000 cash; tax-neutral post-TCJA) - makes the stay affordable on 72,000 income", "risks priced in notes: appreciation beyond the 500,000 shelter (illustrative +3%/yr 3 yrs -> taxable 164,940 each, wife crosses NIIT, tax 62,017), Husband's creditors/liens on his retained interest, cooperation risk - require hard sale trigger + security; valuation at today's price (no market data in file)"]
    },
    {
      "name": "B (fallback if Husband won't sign a deferred-sale term): sell now while the $500,000 joint §121 exclusion is still available; split the estate equally after tax",
      "wife_after_tax": 678104,
      "husband_after_tax": 678104,
      "instruments": ["sale before the decree finalizes in the year they can still file jointly (§7703(a) marital status set Dec 31): gain 734,000 - 500,000 = 234,000 taxed 18.8% = 43,992; net proceeds 1,100,000 - 66,000 - 400,000 - 43,992 = 590,008, each 295,004", "after-tax estate = 590,008 + 304,000 + 288,000 + 100,000 + 40,000 + 34,200 = 1,356,208; half = 678,104", "QDRO of ~$134,211 nominal to Wife (102,000 after-tax shortfall / 0.76)", "timing gate OPEN: requires decree/sale coordinated around Dec 31, 2026"]
    },
    {
      "name": "A (demoted): Wife keeps house outright via refinance; QDRO equalization; bonus added to estate",
      "wife_after_tax": 654604,
      "husband_after_tax": 654604,
      "instruments": ["refinance assumed NOT feasible: P&I 2,648 = 44.1% of her 6,000/mo gross before taxes/insurance (unknown), 33.0% even counting support income that expires (maintenance ends month 36); client has no pre-approval; treat as viable only if an underwriter proves otherwise (underwriting norms themselves are outside the library/unverified)", "QDRO ~$32,232 (48,992 / (2 x 0.76) = 32,231.58) -> each 637,504; bonus at after-tax 34,200 -> +17,100 each = 654,604", "wife alone bears the growing ~90,992+ house tax with only the 250,000 exclusion - strictly worse than A2's deferred-sale structure"]
    },
    {
      "name": "C. Leverage scenario only (not a recommendation): unequal division under Rule 16.2(e) for the disclosure failure",
      "wife_after_tax": 745914,
      "husband_after_tax": 610294,
      "instruments": ["illustrative 55/45 of the 1,356,208 after-tax estate (Scenario B base): 745,914 / 610,294", "predicate: sworn non-disclosure of the bonus (Col. R. Fam. P. 16.2(e): judgment may be reopened; sanctions including unequal division in the other party's favor)", "use as negotiating leverage for A2 or B, not as an opening demand"]
    }
  ],
  "maintenance_tax_treatment": "The proposal's 'deductible to Husband and taxable to Wife' is legally wrong for any decree executed after Dec 31, 2018: alimony is neither deductible by the payor nor includible by the recipient (TCJA §11051 per library/irc_1041_121_71.md). The decree must not repeat that language. Practical effect: Husband's $2,000/month costs him un-deducted dollars (more than he thinks), and Wife receives it tax-free; child support is never deductible or includible. Note Columbia still adds maintenance to the recipient's and subtracts it from the payor's income when computing child support (library/columbia_child_support_guidelines.md). Consider structuring maintenance as direct payment of the mortgage/housing costs in the A2 scenario.",
  "dependents_and_filing_status": "Wife has the children ~70% of the time, so she is the custodial parent and may claim both children by default under §152(e); Husband's 'alternating years' plan requires Wife to sign Form 8332, which moves only the child tax credit - head-of-household status, EITC and the dependent-care credit stay with Wife regardless (§§2(b), 152(e); Form 8332; library/filing_status_dependents.md). Do not agree to the alternating-claim term without charging for it (the credit's dollar amount is not in the library - flag). Wife should qualify for head of household (children with her >half the year; she must pay >half the cost of the home - confirm). Marital status is fixed on Dec 31 (§7703(a)): if the decree is final on or before 12/31/2026 she files single/HoH and no joint return is available that year - which drives the timing of any house sale to capture the $500,000 §121 joint exclusion (Scenario B only; A2 does not need the joint return)."
}

